  "narrative": "## Biological Interpretation\n\nThe RaMP enrichment analysis identifies two strongly significant pathways disrupted in the treatment group. The dominant signal is **Glycine, serine and threonine metabolism (KEGG map00260)**, with an FDR of 0.0 and a remarkable fold-enrichment of 568.9, driven by six matched compounds from the input list. A second major hit, **Metabolism of amino acids and derivatives (Reactome R-HSA-71291)**, is also highly significant (FDR 0.0), consistent with broader amino acid network dysregulation.\n\n### Key Driver Compounds\n\nFive metabolites anchor the biological story. **L-Methionine (C00073)** is a central sulfur amino acid whose metabolism intersects both enriched pathways. KEGG reaction-pathway analysis confirms it is reachable from **S-Adenosylhomocysteine (C00021)** via S-adenosylmethionine (C00019) in two enzymatic steps — reflecting the active methylation cycle under the treatment condition. L-Methionine also connects to **L-Cysteine (C00097)** via cystathionine (C02430) in four reaction steps, placing cysteine downstream in the transsulfuration cascade. **Ornithine (C00077)** is confirmed as a member of glycine/serine/threonine metabolism and links to **1-Pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid (C03912)** — the cyclized dehydrogenase product of ornithine — in three steps, defining the ornithine-to-proline branch.\n\n### Biological Significance\n\nThe co-enrichment of methionine, cysteine, S-adenosylhomocysteine, ornithine, and 2-ketobutyric acid in glycine/serine/threonine metabolism points to a coordinated perturbation of the sulfur amino acid and polyamine-oxidative branches. Altered S-adenosylhomocysteine is particularly notable: as the obligate product of methyltransferase reactions, its accumulation or depletion directly modulates methyltransferase activity and one-carbon folate metabolism, with downstream consequences for DNA methylation, protein methylation, and antioxidant defense. Meanwhile, ornithine and its downstream proline derivative 1-pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid reflect perturbations in arginine/ornithine interconversion, potentially impacting collagen synthesis and urea cycle flux. The involvement of (R)-lipoic acid — a cofactor for dehydrogenases bridging several of these networks — and 2-ketobutyric acid, a central ketoacid node in amino acid catabolism, together suggest that the treatment broadly impairs amino acid catabolic flux and energy metabolism, possibly through mitochondrial dehydrogenase dysfunction. Further investigation of folate cycle intermediates and oxidative stress markers would clarify the directionality of these changes in a disease or physiological context.",
